+
+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The :func:`~rich.progress.open` function reads a file while showing a progress display of how many bytes have been consumed. This is useful for long-running consumers such as a JSON or CSV parser. Pass a path (a ``str``, a path-like object, or ``bytes``). The default mode is text, so reads return ``str``; pass ``'rb'`` to read bytes instead. The display starts when the ``with`` block is entered and stops when it exits. The default ``total`` is the size of the file in bytes::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with rich.progress.open("data.csv") as text_file:
+        for line in text_file:
+            ...
+
+    with rich.progress.open("data.bin", "rb") as binary_file:
+        data = binary_file.read()
+
+If you already have a Progress display, :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open` attaches a reader to it instead. It returns the reader without starting the display, so you can use it in a ``with`` statement yourself::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    progress = rich.progress.Progress()
+    with progress:
+        task_id = progress.add_task("Parsing")
+        with progress.open("data.csv", task_id=task_id) as text_file:
+            ...
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have an open binary file, use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file`. It accepts only binary file-like objects, and you must give the size of the file in bytes as ``total``. The wrapped file is not closed when the ``with`` block exits, so you still own it and must close it yourself::
+
+    import io
+    import rich.progress
+
+    source = io.BytesIO(b"some binary data")
+    with rich.progress.wrap_file(source, 16) as reader:
+        data = reader.read()
+    source.close()
+
+To attach a file-like object to an existing task, use :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file` instead. If the task already has a ``total``, you can omit the ``total`` argument; otherwise a ``total`` is required and a ``ValueError`` is raised::
+
+    import io
+    import rich.progress
+
+    progress = rich.progress.Progress()
+    task_id = progress.add_task("Reading", total=16)
+    source = io.BytesIO(b"some binary data")
+    reader = progress.wrap_file(source, task_id=task_id)
+    data = reader.read()
+    source.close()
+
